@page "/file-view"

<PageTitle>File view — Backlog UI Storybook</PageTitle>

<StoryPage Title="File view"
           Summary="`FileView` is the container over the two halves a file pane is made of: `FileHeader`, which says which file this is and stays put, and `FileContent`, which holds the file and scrolls. Its own job is the two things neither half can do — it does the reading, and it arbitrates the modes. Reading is why the mode question is even answerable: the pane parses the file, reads its frontmatter, resolves its language, takes the file's own metadata block out of the body, and aligns two versions of it, each cached against the text it came from. Arbitration is the rest: edit, compare and read are exclusive, the host's body outranks the pane's, and a control is offered only where pressing it would do something. Every one of those answers has to be the same answer in the header and in the body, and the only way to guarantee that is for one component to give it — neither half decides anything, and neither reads the file. What either half then *draws* is documented on its own page — *File header* and *File content* directly above."
           Guidelines="@Rules">

    <Story Name="What the file is read as"
           Description="The pane reads, so the pane decides. A name the highlighter knows is shown as source — coloured, numbered, and never run through the markdown parser, because a `#` in a shell script is a comment and reading it as a heading is how a file view loses the file. A name it does not know is shown as markdown, which is also what a file of plain prose wants. Nothing at the call site says which is which.

`Language` is the override, for the files whose name carries no grammar: a Dockerfile has no extension, and a build script's `.txt` says nothing about what is in it. It works the other way too — `Language=&quot;markdown&quot;` asks for prose back from a file whose extension would have made it code. A language the highlighter has no grammar for still renders as code in one colour; losing the colour is not a reason to lose the line numbers and reflow the file as prose.

Both halves are told the answer rather than asked for one. `FileContent` takes `RendersMarkdown` and a resolved `Language`; `FileHeader` takes the resolved `Kind`. A header naming a file as a stylesheet over a body that had decided to render it as prose is exactly the disagreement this arrangement makes impossible."
           Code="@ReadingsCode">
        <div class="sb-stack" data-testid="file-view-kinds">
            <p class="sb-label">Read from the name — source, source, prose</p>
            <div class="sb-columns">
                <FileView Name="MarkdownPreview.cs"
                          Path="src\Core\Backlog.UI.Components\Markdown\MarkdownPreview.cs"
                          SizeInBytes="1204"
                          Body="@FileFixtures.CSharpBody"
                          MaxHeight="12rem"
                          TestId="file-view-csharp" />

                <FileView Name="appsettings.json"
                          Path="src\harness\Backlog.UI.Storybook\appsettings.json"
                          SizeInBytes="286"
                          Body="@FileFixtures.JsonBody"
                          MaxHeight="12rem"
                          TestId="file-view-json" />

                <FileView Name="README.md"
                          Path="src\harness\README.md"
                          SizeInBytes="412"
                          Body="@FileFixtures.ShortBody"
                          MaxHeight="12rem"
                          TestId="file-view-markdown" />
            </div>

            <p class="sb-label">Overridden — a name with no grammar to read, and one whose extension is on the list</p>
            <div class="sb-columns" data-testid="file-view-language-override">
                <FileView Name="Dockerfile"
                          Language="bash"
                          SizeInBytes="188"
                          Body="@FileFixtures.DockerfileBody"
                          MaxHeight="12rem"
                          TestId="file-view-dockerfile" />

                <FileView Name="lifecycle.mmd"
                          SizeInBytes="240"
                          Body="@FileFixtures.MermaidBody"
                          MaxHeight="12rem"
                          TestId="file-view-mermaid" />
            </div>
        </div>
    </Story>

    <Story Name="The header stays, the body takes the rest"
           Description="The other thing only the container can settle: how much of the pane each half gets. `Fill` takes the whole height the host gave it — here a 32rem example area — and the body takes whatever is left under a header that never moves. So how tall a file gets is the layout's business rather than a number carried inside the component.

`min-height: 0` is what makes it work: without it a flex child refuses to shrink below its content and the body would push the card open instead of scrolling inside it. Editing takes the same bargain, and asks for it unconditionally — a height of `100%` inside a parent with no definite height resolves to `auto`, which is exactly the content-sized box a host using `MaxHeight` already had.

Tab into the body and the arrow keys scroll it. That the region is a tab stop at all is `FileContent`'s decision, and it is documented there; what is here is that there is one region and one header, and which of them gives."
           Code="@FillCode">
        <div class="sb-tall" data-testid="file-view-long">
            <FileView Name="context-loading.instructions.md"
                      Path=".github\instructions\context-loading.instructions.md"
                      Source="GitHub Copilot"
                      Kind="Path-specific instructions"
                      SizeInBytes="5837"
                      Body="@FileFixtures.LongBody"
                      Fill="true"
                      TestId="file-view-instructions" />
        </div>
    </Story>

    <Story Name="The file's own block goes to the header"
           Description="Two `meta` blocks in one file, and they are not the same fact. The one under the `#` title describes the file, so it is drawn in the header beside the name — where it stays while the body scrolls. The one under each `##` describes that chapter, so it stays with the chapter, folded into its heading.

Which of the two a block is, is a reading, and the reading is the container's: the pane parses the file once, hands the first block to the header, and tells the body to leave it out. Drawn once — not left in the body as a second record, and not falling back to the raw fence either. The title under it is a title again.

No new parameter buys this. `RenderKnowledgeMetadata` is the same opt-in the chapters are read with, and `RenderKnowledgeMetadataFields` governs the header's second tier exactly as it governs a chapter's — the pair of panes below is that one flag, set both ways over the same file. Scroll either of them: the chapters' records leave with their chapters, and the file's does not."
           Code="@FileMetadataCode"
           Source="@FileFixtures.TwoLevelFile">
        <div class="sb-stack" data-testid="file-view-file-metadata">
            <div class="sb-columns">
                <div class="sb-stack">
                    <p class="sb-label">The record whole — the file's fields under its name</p>
                    <FileView Name="shared.md"
                              Kind="Technology stack"
                              Path=".tech/shared.md"
                              Body="@FileFixtures.TwoLevelFile"
                              MaxHeight="22rem"
                              TestId="file-view-two-level"
                              AllowCopy="true"
                              RenderKnowledgeMetadata="true"
                              KnowledgeFolder="KnowledgeFolder.Tech"
                              OnKnowledgeStatusChanged="HearFileStatus" />
                </div>
                <div class="sb-stack">
                    <p class="sb-label">Reading it — the status alone, at both levels</p>
                    <FileView Name="shared.md"
                              Kind="Technology stack"
                              Path=".tech/shared.md"
                              Body="@FileFixtures.TwoLevelFile"
                              MaxHeight="22rem"
                              TestId="file-view-two-level-reading"
                              RenderKnowledgeMetadata="true"
                              RenderKnowledgeMetadataFields="false"
                              KnowledgeFolder="KnowledgeFolder.Tech" />
                </div>
            </div>

            <Alert Message="@(_fileStatus is null ? null : $"Asked to write status: {_fileStatus} to the file's own block.")" Role="status" />
        </div>
    </Story>

    <Story Name="The frontmatter is read once, here"
           Description="`ShowFrontmatter` is another reading: the pane reads the YAML block a markdown file may open with, hands the result to the body as a value, and hands the body the text *without* those lines. Left in, the same four lines come back underneath as the divider and run-together paragraph the markdown parser makes of them, which is the noise this replaces.

Handing the block over as a value rather than a flag is what makes the removal safe: the strip draws every key the block states, including the ones it has no shape for, so nothing leaves the body that the strip does not show. What the strip then looks like — the description as prose, the globs as badges, an unrecognised `mode` as a row of its own — is `FileContent`'s, and it is on that page.

A host that renders its own body names `FrontmatterSource` instead, so the strip is drawn from the file's text while the buffer that host writes back stays whole. A file with no frontmatter, or one nobody asked about, renders exactly as it did before any of this — no strip, and no empty row where it would have been."
           Code="@FrontmatterCode">
        <div class="sb-stack" data-testid="file-view-frontmatter">
            <FileView Name="ui-components.instructions.md"
                      Source="GitHub Copilot"
                      Kind="Path-specific instructions"
                      Path=".github\instructions\ui-components.instructions.md"
                      Body="@FileFixtures.InstructionFile"
                      ShowFrontmatter="true"
                      MaxHeight="12rem"
                      TestId="file-view-instruction-file" />

            <FileView Name="explain-flow.prompt.md"
                      Source="GitHub Copilot"
                      Kind="Prompt file"
                      Path=".github\prompts\explain-flow.prompt.md"
                      Body="@FileFixtures.PromptFile"
                      ShowFrontmatter="true"
                      MaxHeight="12rem"
                      TestId="file-view-prompt" />
        </div>
    </Story>

    <Story Name="Edit, compare, read — one at a time"
           Description="The arbitration, on the file that prompted it. `CanEdit` with `@@bind-Editing` swaps the body for the one `EditBodyContent` supplies; `@@bind-Comparing` with `CompareBaselines` swaps it for two versions aligned by heading. The three modes are exclusive and the pane enforces that, which is why the header's controls ask rather than tell — press Edit while comparing and the comparison closes.

`OffersEdit` and `OffersCompare` fall out of the same arbitration: they answer “would pressing this do anything?”, not “may this file be edited?”. Editing needs a writable body to switch to or a host listening for the switch; comparing needs a baseline that differs from the file. Both are handed to the header already answered, because a header that worked them out for itself could offer a control the body will not honour.

Everything else on this pane is a reading being passed down: each chapter's status where its heading is, the mermaid fence where it was written, a copy button per heading, remarks in the margin falling back to inline when the pane narrows. A host never has to parse the same text a second time to draw those around the outside.

The remarks themselves take four callbacks: `OnAddComment` for the affordance on a block, `OnEditComment` and `OnResolveComment` for an existing remark's own buttons, and `OnDeleteComment` for taking one away outright. Press the affordance and the new remark opens straight into its own textarea — the host answers with an empty body, and an empty body is what tells the view this is a fresh draft rather than something to read. Cancel on that draft calls `OnDeleteComment` too, so an abandoned remark does not linger as a blank note in the margin."
           Code="@ChapterCode">
        <div class="sb-tall" data-testid="file-view-knowledge">
            <FileView Name="Domain: Tasks"
                      Kind="Domain model narrative"
                      Path=".domain/tasks/domain.md"
                      Body="@_domain"
                      Fill="true"
                      TestId="file-view-domain"
                      AllowCopy="true"
                      AllowChapterCopy="true"
                      RenderKnowledgeMetadata="true"
                      KnowledgeFolder="KnowledgeFolder.Domain"
                      OnKnowledgeStatusChanged="OnStatusChanged"
                      Comments="@_remarks"
                      OnAddComment="AddRemark"
                      OnResolveComment="ResolveRemark"
                      OnEditComment="EditRemark"
                      OnDeleteComment="DeleteRemark"
                      CanEdit="true"
                      @bind-Editing="_editing"
                      @bind-Comparing="_comparing"
                      @bind-CompareBaselineId="_baseline"
                      CompareBaselines="@Baselines">
                <EditBodyContent>
                    @* Fill, because this pane has a height and the editor should
                       take it: Rows is what the editor falls back to when nobody
                       has told it how tall to be, and here somebody has. Without
                       it the reader presses Edit on a full-height pane and gets a
                       sixteen-row box with the rest of the pane empty under it. *@
                    <MarkdownEditor Value="@_domain"
                                    ValueChanged="value => _domain = value"
                                    AriaLabel="Chapter source"
                                    Rows="16"
                                    Fill="true"
                                    Bare="true"
                                    TestId="file-view-domain-editor" />
                </EditBodyContent>
            </FileView>
        </div>
    </Story>

    <Story Name="A control nobody would gain anything by pressing"
           Description="The same arbitration, from the other side. Compare is absent here, and that is the story: the only baseline says exactly what the file says, so pressing it would show the reader the file they are already looking at. The pane knows because the pane holds the baselines and does the aligning; the header is simply told there is nothing to offer.

The rest of this pane is what the knowledge panes ask for. `RenderKnowledgeMetadataFields` off keeps the status — the one part of a `meta` block with a reading behind it — and drops the rows under it, which are the fence again with the fence taken off. A `.domain/…md` written in the prose becomes the same reference the record draws, through the same `OnKnowledgeNavigate` — press one and this story reports it."
           Code="@ReadingCode">
        <div class="sb-tall" data-testid="file-view-reading">
            <FileView Name="Domain: Tasks"
                      Kind="Domain model narrative"
                      Path=".domain/tasks/domain.md"
                      Body="@FileFixtures.ReadingChapter"
                      Fill="true"
                      TestId="file-view-reading-example"
                      AllowCopy="true"
                      AllowChapterCopy="true"
                      RenderKnowledgeMetadata="true"
                      RenderKnowledgeMetadataFields="false"
                      KnowledgeFolder="KnowledgeFolder.Domain"
                      OnKnowledgeNavigate="reference => _followed = reference.Raw"
                      CompareBaselines="@UnchangedBaseline" />

            <Alert Message="@(_followed is null ? null : $"Asked to open {_followed}.")" Role="status" />
        </div>
    </Story>

    <Story Name="Two versions, aligned by the pane"
           Description="Aligning is the reading the comparison needs, and it happens here: the pane walks both documents once, holds the result, and hands it down — a pane re-renders every time a fold in it opens, so aligning in the body would redo that walk on each pass.

What a baseline *is* stays the host's. This page holds two strings; the domain panel holds one and asks git for the other, which is why `FileCompareBaseline` is a record rather than a callback — a pane that knew how to fetch one version would have to know how to fetch all of them. A baseline with nothing to offer says why: press Compare, then pick “Never committed”.

The drawing is `MarkdownCompareView`'s, handed the result `Bare` so it gives up its own card, header and scroll region — this pane already has all three. That side of it is on *File content*."
           Code="@CompareCode">
        <div class="sb-tall" data-testid="file-view-compare">
            <FileView Name="context-map.md"
                      Kind="Domain context map"
                      Path=".domain/context-map.md"
                      Body="@FileFixtures.ContextMapAfter"
                      Fill="true"
                      TestId="file-view-compare-example"
                      Comparing="true"
                      CompareBaselines="@CompareExamples"
                      @bind-CompareBaselineId="_exampleBaseline" />
        </div>
    </Story>

    <Story Name="A body the host brings itself"
           Description="`BodyContent` hands the body in, and the precedence is the pane's: a host body outranks both built-in shapes, its *editing* body outranks its ordinary one while a reader is editing, and a comparison outranks all three — because a comparison is the pane's own reading of the file and a host's editor has nothing to say about the version that came before. `FileContent` is handed one fragment, already chosen.

What the host keeps is everything the container is: the frame, the identity, the path, the actions and the one scroll region. Type in it and watch the size in the header move — what the file now says is the host's to report, and the pane draws what it is told.

Tab through this one and then the arbitration story above. A supplied body gives up the region's `tabindex` and the built-in shapes keep it; that consequence, and why, is on *File content*."
           Code="@HostedBodyCode">
        <div class="sb-tall" data-testid="file-view-hosted-body">
            <FileView Name="05-building-block-view.md"
                      Path=".arc42\05-building-block-view.md"
                      Source="Repository"
                      Kind="arc42 chapter"
                      SizeInBytes="@ChapterSize"
                      Fill="true"
                      TestId="file-view-hosted">
                <BodyContent>
                    <MarkdownEditor Value="@_chapter"
                                    ValueChanged="value => _chapter = value"
                                    AriaLabel="Chapter source"
                                    Rows="12"
                                    TestId="file-view-hosted-editor" />
                </BodyContent>
            </FileView>
        </div>
    </Story>

</StoryPage>

@code {
    private static readonly DesignGuideline[] Rules =
    [
        new("content-editing.md#knowledge-metadata-blocks", "Which of a file's metadata blocks is drawn where."),
        new("content-editing.md#raw-markdown-escape-hatch", "When a file is shown as source rather than as a rendering."),
        new("color-scheme.md#syntax-highlighting-tokens", "The colours a file read as code is drawn with."),
        new("design-principles.md#low-chrome-content-first", "How much of a file view may be header and actions."),
        new("typography-and-layout.md#layout-grid-and-breakpoints", "The measure a file's contents are read at.")
    ];

    // What the header's record reported. The file below is a constant, so nothing
    // is written back into it: which file, which fence and what happens when the
    // file has moved on is the host's, and this host is a page showing you that
    // the callback fired.
    private string? _fileStatus;

    private void HearFileStatus(KnowledgeStatusChange change) => _fileStatus = change.Status;

    private const string FileMetadataCode = """
        @* One opt-in, two placements: the pane reads the file once and knows which
           block describes the file and which describes a chapter. The header keeps
           the first; the body keeps the rest, where the chapters are. *@
        <FileView Name="shared.md"
                  Kind="Technology stack"
                  Path=".tech/shared.md"
                  Body="@markdown"
                  MaxHeight="22rem"
                  AllowCopy="true"
                  RenderKnowledgeMetadata="true"
                  KnowledgeFolder="KnowledgeFolder.Tech"
                  OnKnowledgeStatusChanged="HearStatus" />
        """;

    // The one piece of state on this page: the hosted-body story is only worth
    // looking at if it can actually be typed in.
    private string _chapter = FileFixtures.ChapterSource;

    // The knowledge story's state. Editing and comparing are the component's
    // modes; the rest is what a host owns — the text, the remarks, and which
    // version is being compared against.
    private string _domain = FileFixtures.DomainChapter;
    private bool _editing;
    private bool _comparing;
    private string _baseline = "opened";
    private string _exampleBaseline = "commit";
    private int _nextRemark = 3;

    private IReadOnlyList<MarkdownComment> _remarks =
    [
        new("r1", 2, "Is \"triaged\" the word we settled on, or is it still \"refined\"?", "Reviewer", "yesterday"),
        new("r2", 6, "This invariant is the one the writer enforces — worth naming it here.", "You", "2 hours ago", Resolved: true)
    ];

    /// <summary>Two versions to compare the chapter against. "As opened" is the
    /// text this page loaded with, which is what "what have I changed" means on a
    /// surface that auto-saves; the committed one is a fixture, because a
    /// storybook has no repository behind it.</summary>
    private IReadOnlyList<FileCompareBaseline> Baselines =>
    [
        new("opened", "As opened", FileFixtures.DomainChapter),
        new("committed", "Last commit", FileFixtures.CommittedDomainChapter)
    ];

    /// <summary>What the reading story reports when a reference in its prose is
    /// followed. A pane would change what is on screen; a storybook has nowhere to
    /// go, so it says where it was asked to go instead.</summary>
    private string? _followed;

    /// <summary>One baseline, holding exactly what the file holds. Which is why
    /// the story has no Compare button: there is nothing on the other side of it.</summary>
    private IReadOnlyList<FileCompareBaseline> UnchangedBaseline =>
        [new("opened", "As opened", FileFixtures.ReadingChapter)];

    private IReadOnlyList<FileCompareBaseline> CompareExamples =>
    [
        new("commit", "Last commit", FileFixtures.ContextMapBefore),
        new("empty", "Never committed", null, "This chapter has not been committed yet, so there is no earlier version of it to compare with.")
    ];

    /// <summary>A status change reports the block it came from and the chapter
    /// title, which is what lets a host find the file's own heading again. Written
    /// into the text here so the story keeps telling the truth about itself.</summary>
    private void OnStatusChanged(KnowledgeStatusChange change) =>
        _domain = _domain.Replace($"status: {StatusOf(change.Heading)}", $"status: {change.Status}", StringComparison.Ordinal);

    /// <summary>What the fence under a heading currently says, so the replacement
    /// above rewrites that chapter's line and not the first one in the file.</summary>
    private string StatusOf(string? heading)
    {
        var lines = _domain.Replace("\r\n", "\n").Split('\n');
        var start = Array.FindIndex(lines, line => heading is not null && line.TrimStart('#', ' ') == heading);
        if (start < 0) return "draft";

        var status = lines.Skip(start + 1).Take(4).FirstOrDefault(line => line.TrimStart().StartsWith("status:", StringComparison.Ordinal));
        return status?.Split(':', 2)[1].Trim() ?? "draft";
    }

    /// <summary>Empty, so the read view opens it straight into its own textarea —
    /// the reader types where they pressed rather than into a placeholder they
    /// would have had to find Edit to reach.</summary>
    private void AddRemark(int blockIndex) =>
        _remarks = [.. _remarks, new MarkdownComment($"r{_nextRemark++}", blockIndex, string.Empty, "You", "just now")];

    private void ResolveRemark(string id) =>
        _remarks = [.. _remarks.Select(remark => remark.Id == id ? remark with { Resolved = !remark.Resolved } : remark)];

    private void EditRemark(MarkdownComment edited) =>
        _remarks = [.. _remarks.Select(remark => remark.Id == edited.Id ? edited : remark)];

    /// <summary>Taken away outright — a saved remark's own Delete button, or a
    /// fresh draft Cancel is abandoning rather than saving.</summary>
    private void DeleteRemark(string id) =>
        _remarks = [.. _remarks.Where(remark => remark.Id != id)];

    // Read off the text rather than written down, so the header keeps telling the
    // truth while the story is being edited. A host that owns the body owns
    // saying what the file is now.
    private long ChapterSize => System.Text.Encoding.UTF8.GetByteCount(_chapter);

    private const string FillCode = """
        @* Fill needs a container with a height to fill — in a flex or grid
           layout that is whatever track the file lands in. *@
        <div style="height: 32rem">
            <FileView Name="context-loading.instructions.md"
                      Path=".github\instructions\context-loading.instructions.md"
                      Source="GitHub Copilot"
                      Kind="Path-specific instructions"
                      SizeInBytes="5837"
                      Body="@body"
                      Fill="true" />
        </div>
        """;

    private const string ReadingsCode = """
        @* Nothing says which is which: the name does, and the pane reads it. *@
        <FileView Name="MarkdownPreview.cs" Body="@source" MaxHeight="12rem" />
        <FileView Name="appsettings.json" Body="@config" MaxHeight="12rem" />
        <FileView Name="README.md" Body="@readme" MaxHeight="12rem" />

        @* Language is the override, for a name with no grammar to read — and for
           the other direction, prose out of a file whose extension says code. *@
        <FileView Name="Dockerfile" Language="bash" Body="@dockerfile" MaxHeight="12rem" />
        <FileView Name="notes.cs" Language="markdown" Body="@notes" MaxHeight="12rem" />
        """;

    private const string HostedBodyCode = """
        @* The body is the host's. The frame, the name, the path and the scroll
           region are not — which is the reason to reach for FileView here
           instead of building a header that looks like one. *@
        <FileView Name="05-building-block-view.md"
                  Path=".arc42\05-building-block-view.md"
                  Kind="arc42 chapter"
                  SizeInBytes="@size"
                  Fill="true">
            <BodyContent>
                <MarkdownEditor Value="@source" ValueChanged="OnChanged" Rows="12" />
            </BodyContent>
        </FileView>
        """;

    private const string ChapterCode = """
        @* One component. The file decides what the body shows — a status where a
           `meta` fence is, a diagram where a diagram fence is — and the header
           decides which of the three modes it is in. *@
        <FileView Name="Domain: Tasks"
                  Path=".domain/tasks/domain.md"
                  Body="@markdown"
                  Fill="true"
                  AllowCopy="true"
                  AllowChapterCopy="true"
                  RenderKnowledgeMetadata="true"
                  KnowledgeFolder="KnowledgeFolder.Domain"
                  OnKnowledgeStatusChanged="OnStatusChanged"
                  Comments="@remarks"
                  OnAddComment="Add"
                  OnResolveComment="Resolve"
                  OnDeleteComment="Delete"
                  CanEdit="true"
                  @bind-Editing="editing"
                  @bind-Comparing="comparing"
                  CompareBaselines="@baselines">
            <EditBodyContent>
                @* Fill, because the pane above already has a height: Rows is the
                   fallback for an editor nobody sized, and one here would leave
                   dead space under the last row. *@
                <MarkdownEditor Value="@markdown" ValueChanged="OnChanged" Rows="16" Fill="true" Bare="true" />
            </EditBodyContent>
        </FileView>
        """;

    private const string ReadingCode = """
        @* A reading surface: the status is kept and the bookkeeping under it is
           not, the diagram carries no fold, and a chapter named in the prose goes
           through the same navigation the metadata references already use. *@
        <FileView Name="Domain: Tasks"
                  Path=".domain/tasks/domain.md"
                  Body="@markdown"
                  Fill="true"
                  AllowCopy="true"
                  AllowChapterCopy="true"
                  RenderKnowledgeMetadata="true"
                  RenderKnowledgeMetadataFields="false"
                  KnowledgeFolder="KnowledgeFolder.Domain"
                  OnKnowledgeNavigate="Open"
                  CompareBaselines="@baselines" />
        """;

    private const string CompareCode = """
        @* Reading a version is the host's — a string it kept, or a blob it asked
           git for. A baseline with nothing to offer says why instead. *@
        private IReadOnlyList<FileCompareBaseline> Baselines =>
        [
            new("commit", "Last commit", committedText),
            new("empty", "Never committed", null, "This chapter has not been committed yet.")
        ];
        """;

    private const string FrontmatterCode = """
        @* Opt-in, and the body loses the block only where the strip is showing it.
           A host drawing its own body names the text instead, so the buffer it
           writes back keeps every line it arrived with. *@
        <FileView Name="ui-components.instructions.md"
                  Path=".github\instructions\ui-components.instructions.md"
                  Body="@markdown"
                  ShowFrontmatter="true" />

        <FileView Name="ui-components.instructions.md"
                  ShowFrontmatter="true"
                  FrontmatterSource="@markdown">
            <BodyContent>
                <MarkdownEditor Value="@markdown" ValueChanged="OnChanged" Rows="12" />
            </BodyContent>
        </FileView>
        """;
}
